            0.119,
            0.072,
            0.159,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "140"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.071,
            0.494,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.11,
            0.881,
            0.169
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let the radius of the sphere be r. The surface area of the complete sphere is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "4 \\pi r ^ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Therefore, the curved surface area of a hemisphere, which is half of the sphere, is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 \\pi r ^ { 2 }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.173,
            0.881,
            0.213
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The total surface area (TSA) includes the curved surface and circular base. The area of the circular bas "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathbf { e } = \\pi r ^ { 2 }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.217,
            0.473,
            0.237
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hence "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { T S A } = 2 \\pi r ^ { 2 } + \\pi r ^ { 2 } = 3 \\pi r ^ { 2 } ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.245,
            0.521,
            0.28
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Volume of the hemisphere "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "=   { \\frac { 2 } { 3 } }   \\pi r ^ { 3 } ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.284,
            0.881,
            0.323
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 4:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " A hemispherical bowl has a radius of 3.5 cm. What would be the volume of water it would contain?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.323,
            0.613,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The volume of water the bowl can contain"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.296,
            0.351,
            0.701,
            0.426
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_17_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}=&\\frac{2}{3} \\pi r^{3} \\\\=&\\frac{2}{3} \\times \\frac{22}{7} \\times 3.5 \\times 3.5 \\times 3.5  cm ^{3} = 89.8  cm ^{3}.\\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.366,
            0.455,
            0.632,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise Set 14.4"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.503,
            0.816,
            0.525
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. A ball bearing has a radius of 0.7 cm. Find its surface area."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.528,
            0.882,
            0.588
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "*2. Two solid spheres made of the same metal have weights 5920 g and 740 g. Determine the radius of the larger sphere, if the diameter of the smaller one is 5 cm."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.592,
            0.882,
            0.652
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. The diameter of the moon is approximately one fourth the diameter of the earth. Given that the moon and earth are both roughly spherical, find the ratio of their surface areas."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.655,
            0.882,
            0.695
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Find (i) the curved surface area and (ii) the total surface area of a hemisphere of radius 21 cm."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.699,
            0.883,
            0.797
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Metal spheres, each of radius 2 cm, are packed into a rectangular box of internal dimensions 16 cm × 8 cm × 8 cm. When 16 spheres are packed, the box is filled with preservative liquid. Find the volume of this liquid. Round your answer to the nearest integer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.8,
            0.881,
            0.84
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. The radius of a sphere is increased by 10%. Show that the volume increases by approximately 33.1%."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
